WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.640
We will now see how we can use

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:08.480
tests to examine
the distribution of our sample.

3
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:12.080
Starting on the left, we test whether

4
00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.440
our distribution is Gaussian, or normal.

5
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.680
We have a randomly drawn sample,

6
00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.820
shown here as a histogram,

7
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:26.160
which we can visually compare
with a normal distribution curve

8
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.240
with the same mean and standard
deviation as our sample.

9
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.580
We use the Shapiro–Wilcoxon
test, with its W statistic.

10
00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.900
For our sample, the value of W is 0.976.

11
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.420
We cannot interpret this value on its own,

12
00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:48.700
but it has an associated
probability of wrongly rejecting H0

13
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:54.900
when H0 is true: 0.146,

14
00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.800
or a 14.6% risk of being
wrong if we reject H0.

15
00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.980
That risk is too high,
so we decide not to reject H0:

16
00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:09.760
that this distribution is normal.

17
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.660
We therefore accept the hypothesis

18
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:18.780
that it does follow a normal distribution.

19
00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:23.400
We can also look at graphs to assess this,

20
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.380
such as a normal Q–Q plot.

21
00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:31.600
Here we compare the distribution
of our observations with a

22
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:37.440
theoretical normal distribution.
The more closely the sample's blue points

23
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:43.160
follow the theoretical line,
the more clearly the plot suggests

24
00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.680
that our sample follows
a normal distribution.

25
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:51.740
For samples that follow
a normal distribution,

26
00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:57.840
we can then use our sample

27
00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.600
in other types of tests,
known as parametric tests,

28
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.260
which were developed
for normally distributed data.

29
00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.040
If, instead, as on the right, our sample

30
00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.180
has been generated
from a log-normal distribution,

31
00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.400
the Wilcoxon test gives
a very small p-value,

32
00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:23.660
of 10⁻⁶. We therefore
reject H0 that the sample

33
00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:28.200
follows a normal distribution,
as the Q–Q plot confirms.

34
00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:34.280
We reject normality for this sample,

35
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.400
and turn instead to non-parametric tests.
